# What the Docker build is allowed to see. Used by `docker build -f apps/api/Dockerfile .`;
# the daemon reads this file from the context root, which is why it lives here and not beside
# the Dockerfile.
#
# ## Why this is an allow-list
#
# `audit.projectors.ts` makes the same argument for the same reason (decision D-35): a deny-list
# keeps its promise only until somebody adds a file nobody thought to exclude. Here the file
# nobody thought to exclude is `.env`, or a `.pem` a future certbot step drops in the repository,
# and the consequence is worse than an audit entry with too many columns - a secret copied into
# an image layer is in every registry that ever pulled it, is visible to anyone who can run
# `docker history`, and cannot be rotated by editing a file.
#
# So: exclude everything, then name what the build genuinely needs. Adding a workspace package
# means adding a line here, and forgetting produces ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE on the next
# build - loud, immediate, and fixed in a minute. That is the right direction for this mistake
# to fail in.
#
# Last rule wins in Docker, unlike .gitignore. The order below is therefore load-bearing.

# 1. Nothing.
*

# 2. The workspace definition. `--frozen-lockfile` reads all four of these and resolves nothing
#    without them; `.npmrc` in particular carries auto-install-peers, so leaving it out gives
#    the image a different dependency graph from the one that was tested.
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!.npmrc

# 3. The source. Every workspace manifest has to be present for the lockfile to validate, even
#    for packages this image never builds.
!apps
!packages

# 4. ...minus the two SPAs and the component library, which the API image does not build. Their
#    manifests stay, because step 3's rule about the lockfile still applies to them. `apps/web/*`
#    matches direct children only - Docker's `*` does not cross a slash - which is exactly what
#    is wanted: the directory itself stays visible so the exception below can reach into it.
apps/web/*
!apps/web/package.json
apps/admin/*
!apps/admin/package.json
packages/ui/*
!packages/ui/package.json

# 5. ...and minus everything generated. `dist` is rebuilt inside the image on purpose: shipping a
#    Windows-built bundle into a Linux container is how a build stops proving anything. Sending
#    `node_modules` would be worse - it holds the win32 binaries of sharp and argon2, and pnpm
#    would then be reconciling a tree it did not create.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.turbo
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# 6. The last word, whatever the rules above say or a future edit to them says. Every one of
#    these is already excluded by rule 1 and none is re-included by rules 2-5; they are written
#    out anyway because this is the block that has to hold when somebody widens an allow-list in
#    a hurry, and a rule that is only redundant while the file is correct is not redundant.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/id_rsa*
.git
.services
